// Copy this file to Local.xcconfig (gitignored, next to this one) and fill in both values to build Cirruscope with
// your own team. An Apple ID signed in to Xcode is enough: a free Apple Developer account provisions everything the
// app uses, the App Group included. The maintainer's own builds need no Local.xcconfig at all.
//
// Both values are needed because the shipping bundle identifiers, and the App Group derived from them, are registered
// to the maintainer's team, and no other team can claim them. Every identifier the project uses — the apps', the
// widget extension's, the App Group's, the Keychain service's, the Keychain access group's and the background task's —
// derives from the base one.
//
// A Local.xcconfig made from an earlier version of this file, which switched signing to Manual and named a
// provisioning profile and the entitlements file, must be replaced rather than kept: those assignments now reach
// the widget extension and both test bundles as well, which signs them against a profile made for something else.
// Do not pick a team in a target's Signing & Capabilities tab either. That writes DEVELOPMENT_TEAM into
// project.pbxproj at target level, where it overrides this file and ends up committed.
//
// Cirruscope.xcconfig includes Local.xcconfig last, so whatever you set here overrides its defaults.
// Cirruscope/Cirruscope.xcconfig and every per-target .xcconfig end with the same #include? "Local.xcconfig",
// resolved relative to each file, so a Local.xcconfig in one of their folders would be read as well. Keep it at
// the repository root all the same: every folder holding one of those files is synchronized, so a Local.xcconfig
// placed there is copied into the built product of every target listing that folder, and ignoring it in git does
// not stop that.

DEVELOPMENT_TEAM = <your team ID>
CIRRUSCOPE_BASE_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER = <a reverse-DNS prefix of your own>.cirruscope
